  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sustain Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Smallholder farmers, farmers organizations, Agro-dealers, population</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sudan is a country at war, with immense effects both on the production of food for domestic consumption and of agricultural commodities for exports. To date, about half of the population are suffering from acute and ongoing under- and mal-nutrition, to levels that actually cause starvation. Reports of UN entities, domes c and international humanitarian organizations continue to raise alarms over the humanitarian impacts of the war. While food imports and food aid are vitally important, they can never meet the full need of the Sudanese population. Sudan&#x2019;s agricultural sector has traditionally foreseen in production of most of its grains consumption, as well as in a variety of other products both for domes c processing and consumption, as well as for exports. Sudan&#x2019;s secret was not so much in high yields, but in extensive areale of fertile soils and water access. In other words, farmers in Sudan can produce the food the country needs, if they have access to the usual inputs: seeds, fertilizer, crop protection, fodder, and in some cases also irrigation and mechanization. And supporting all this: an agricultural inputs sector and a financial sector with products and product conditions geared to serve the agricultural sector. Our assumption is that this basically also is the way forward during &#x2013; and hopefully soon emerging from &#x2013; the war &#x2013; through a market-based approach that reactivates actors that traditionally always have helped Sudan&#x2019;s primary agricultural production sector to produce that food. Sudan&#x2019;s farm inputs imports have dramatically dropped during the war. Against a nominal import of about 500,000 Mtons of fertilizer during good years, our estimate of imports in 2024 hovers around 50,000 Mtons &#x2013; a sheer decima on of the import volume. The situation around seeds varies from one place and one crop to another, but also requires attention. The role of Sustain Sudan is to catalyse and re-vitalize the imports and distribution of farm inputs to Sudanese farmers. We do that by engaging with international suppliers through the Sustain Africa network, and with domestic agro-dealers, farmer organizations and agricultural financiers.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">i) increasing the volume of quality fertilizer  supplies to the country; ii) improving the affordability of fertilizer for smallholder farmers; iii) increasing access to  technical assistance iv) increasing availability of seeds and crop protection.</narrative>
  </description>
